.TH WARREN 1
.SH NAME
warren \- a maze game written entirely in the shell
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sh /lib/tiles/warren.sh
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Warren
is a maze: walk it, take the twelve coins, do not walk into walls.
Arrow keys or
.BR wasd ;
.B Esc
or
.B q
stops.
.PP
It is here because of what it is made of.
There is no Limbo in
.B /lib/tiles/warren.sh
and none behind it written for this game:
.IR tiles (2)
draws from named palettes, sheets and surfaces made by text,
its art and its level are files,
and
.IR sh-tiles (1)
forwards commands to it, answers questions in
.BR ${} ,
reads the keyboard through
.IR keyhold (2)
and paces the frames.
What is left over is the rules, and the rules of a maze game are twenty
lines of shell:
.IP
.EX
ks = ${tiles $id keys}
if {~ $ks left}  { nx = ${expr $x 1 \-} }
c = ${tiles $id at level $nx $ny}
if {! ~ $c \(aq#\(aq} {
	x = $nx
	if {~ $c o} {
		tiles $id set level $x $y .
		score = ${expr $score 10 +}
	}
}
tiles $id blit level screen 0 0
tiles $id put screen ${expr $x 8 \(aq*\(aq} ${expr $y 8 \(aq*\(aq} art 6 skin
tiles $id draw screen
tiles $id pace 110
.EE
.PP
The level is
.BR /lib/tiles/warren.map ,
which carries its own legend, and the art is
.BR /lib/tiles/demo.chr ,
which is lines of
.BR ".123" .
Edit either and the game changes with no compiler anywhere in sight.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /lib/tiles/warren.sh
the game
.TP
.B /lib/tiles/warren.map
the level, legend and all
.TP
.B /lib/tiles/demo.chr
the tile art
.SH SOURCE
.B /lib/tiles/warren.sh
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR sh (1),
.IR sh-tiles (1),
.IR tiles (2),
.IR keyhold (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Two hundred unpaced frames of this loop take 44 milliseconds \- 220
microseconds a frame, four and a half thousand a second.
The frame is paced to 110 milliseconds because a maze walked a tile at a
time wants to be walked at a walking speed, not because the shell cannot
go faster.
.SH BUGS
There is one level and nothing chases you.
.PP
The player moves a whole tile a frame, so the pace is the movement
speed: there is no interpolation and there could not easily be one
without the shell doing arithmetic per pixel.
.PP
.B $(...)
would deadlock where
.B ${...}
does not \- a backquote runs its block in a subprocess and a subprocess
reaching into the parent's Tk window hangs.
The script says so where it matters.
.PP
The multiplications are written
.B \(aq*\(aq
because an unquoted
.B *
is a glob, and an unquoted one here quietly multiplied by whatever
happened to be in the current directory.
